{"ts": 0.19188, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;97mNames for places\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nA \u001b[1mpath\u001b[0m names a place. Three short names:\r\n", "len": 81}
{"ts": 0.191913, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "  \u001b[1m.\u001b[0m  here, the current directory    \u001b[1m..\u001b[0m  one level up    \u001b[1m~\u001b[0m  your home folder\r\n\r\nA \u001b[1mrelative\u001b[0m path starts where you are: in ~/projects, the name notes\r\nmeans ~/projects/notes. An \u001b[1mabsolute\u001b[0m path starts from /, the top:\r\n/usr/bin is the same place wherever you stand.\r\n  \u001b[92mcd stage1\u001b[0m      \u001b[90m# step into a folder\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mls ..\u001b[0m          \u001b[90m# look one level up: your practice folder\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mcd ..\u001b[0m          \u001b[90m# go back up\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mecho ~\u001b[0m         \u001b[90m# ~ is a name: echo prints what it stands for\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[93m▶ Try all four, then type \u001b[92mnext\u001b[93m.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 626}
{"ts": 0.19589, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;90mnext\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mshow\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mprev\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mquit\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 71}
